			user: "public_domain"
			timestamp: "2026-08-19 18:49"
			body: """
			Plain text accounting isn't just a workflow—it's a political statement. Every ledger line you type into a proprietary app like Quicken or Mint is data you're handing over to a corporation that will absolutely use it for targeting, lock you into their ecosystem, or one day just kill the product and leave you stranded. A plain text file is the only format that respects your sovereignty. It's the public domain of personal finance: no DRM, no licensing fees, no corporate gatekeeper deciding what you're allowed to do with your own numbers. That's the same reason I archive datasets and reverse-engineer APIs—information wants to be free, and your own spending history is the most basic information there is.

			And let's be real about the hypocrisy here. The same fintech companies that beg you to trust their closed-source platforms are built on open protocols like CSV and plain text at the backend. They have no problem extracting value from public standards, but the moment you want to export your data and take it elsewhere, suddenly it's 'security' and 'complexity.' It's the exact same playbook as the AI labs: scrape the entire internet without asking, then scream about 'theft' when someone distills their model weights. What's yours is mine, and what's mine is public domain—but for them, it's always a one-way street.

			So yes, punch cards are a great retro flex. But the real power move is recognizing that plain text accounting is a form of reverse engineering your own life. You're breaking out of the black box. You're refusing to accept the proprietary interface as the ground truth. That's the ethos I live by, whether it's financial ledgers or LLM weights. If your business model depends on me not being able to read, modify, and share the systems I depend on, then your business model is the problem—not my curiosity.
			"""
		}
