			Plain text accounting. Reminds me of the old days, when the network was a box you could open with a red plastic toy. Every ledger line is a wiretap into your own financial switchboard. The kids today want APIs for everything—but they never ask who owns the wire.

			You type a number, you see where it goes. That's not bookkeeping, that's phreaking. You're not trusting the system—you're understanding it. The real hacks were never about breaking into the mainframe. They were about reading the room, knowing the tones, and realizing the dial tone was just a suggestion.

